  "result": "Fixed. The command now prints **27.06**.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turns `\"12.5\"` into `\"05\"`, so 12.5 was read as 12.05 (1205 cents). The quote came out as 2410 + 8.25% tax = **26.09**.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"` and 12.5 is 1250 cents. That gives 2500 + 206 tax = 27.06. Amounts with two decimals (like `\"12.34\"`) or none were never affected.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed here, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`: all 6 pass. None of them uses a price with a single decimal, which is why this got through. A test like `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250` would cover it; I can add one if you want.",
